#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516929} :activity/id #uuid "2509a6a0-9df2-57e4-aee9-31a2cd4f3649" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048423 :topic/id #uuid "9ddcc703-a984-5097-a142-236dc3c2fbaf" :topic/math-academy-id 2979} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2057272a-a597-5b12-aed1-a3af401bdb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516174} {:answer/id #uuid "5796b45f-c2ac-5d55-b3fc-c9c7d0eeaab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516297} {:answer/id #uuid "669845e1-7425-5a30-8b6e-cf58c8bc05e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516335} {:answer/id #uuid "8501c1b4-2888-5212-9951-757335f5b2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516395} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f09f88-2ade-51fb-8aa2-5f0ea6cac91f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516174} :answer-field/id #uuid "be34ef91-5efd-5e6a-b5e1-ff532d2967ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf4d19a8-5c2e-5168-bc23-2f319900269a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110802" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {10}^{x}-3x\\cdot {10}^{x}+2\\cdot {10}^{x}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {10}^{x}-3x\\cdot {10}^{x}+2\\cdot {10}^{x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-3x+2){10}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+2=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{10}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-3x+2=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x-2)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=1,2.$\n\n- The equation ${10}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${10}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=1,2,$ and their product is $1\\cdot 2=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "82b4b547-400b-5d76-b8a1-fb3d5c269718" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516943}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516943 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6c3f287-5dae-5601-8ebb-1ede42e5ca46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291423} :answer-field/id #uuid "b46a4763-9986-5fe1-adec-09a176e52d03" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292293} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7541a123-c295-5764-92f6-cc633d5666e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291133} :answer-field/id #uuid "d77cc2d2-1e8e-508d-83cc-6c1e4f0ee7f9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a6bdd7ef-3aa2-50b8-bd67-7fd5c008275b" :question/math-academy-id "q-264754" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2x}-50\\cdot {7}^{2x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2x}-50\\cdot {7}^{2x} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-50)\\cdot {7}^{2x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-50=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-50=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-25) & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)(x+5) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${7}^{2x}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{2x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a1e388b6-4b60-5a84-a896-498b3b87a0f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516981}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a78efb5-52b6-5d3c-b5a0-49f39f6da8ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291022} :answer-field/id #uuid "818baac4-9846-582e-a016-d7c37e9a7e42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292135} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a37e3585-e3ba-5017-8c94-4dbab9c04a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291345} :answer-field/id #uuid "885f1080-4f56-5ac6-a8d7-1a1b1f221f7d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f911a188-0d00-5124-b074-90cbb871a900" :question/math-academy-id "q-264780" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+11x\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+28\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+11x\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+28\\cdot {5}^{2x+1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+11x+28){5}^{2x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+11x+28=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{2x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+11x+28=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+7)(x+4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-7,-4.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{2x+1}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{2x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-7\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "edd4ce43-80f9-5f81-818c-9ae2ce5ab4d1"}] :activity/title "Solving Exponential Equations Using the Zero-Product Property" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516090}]}
